[Chapter "Key ideas: Why the Queen's Gambit Declined"]
[Type "ideas"]

{Why play the Queen's Gambit Declined? It is the most solid answer to 2.c4. Black keeps a pawn on d5, develops quickly and castles early, and White has few ways to start an early attack. Nearly every game of the 1927 world championship match between José Raúl Capablanca and Alexander Alekhine began with it, and it is still a main defense at the top.

The price is the c8 bishop, shut in behind the pawn on e6. Much of Black's play is about freeing that bishop or trading it.

Our setup is the Tartakower Variation, also called the Tartakower–Makogonov–Bondarevsky system, or TMB: ...Nf6, ...Be7, castle, ...h6, then ...b6 and ...Bb7. The fianchetto gives the c8 bishop a long diagonal, and ...b6 supports the freeing break ...c5. It was a regular guest in the world championship matches between Anatoly Karpov and Garry Kasparov.

Compare the other replies to 2.c4. 2...dxc4, the Accepted, gives White the center. 2...c6, the Slav, keeps the bishop free but takes c6 away from the knight, and it needs more theory.

Step through the moves to see the idea.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 {[%cal Ge6d5] [%csl Yc8] Black holds d5 with a pawn. If White takes on d5, Black takes back with the e-pawn and keeps a pawn in the center.

White plays 3.Nc3 in six games out of ten and 3.Nf3 in about one in five.}
3. Nc3 Nf6 {Guards d5 again and prepares to castle. Now White chooses between 4.Bg5 (about a third of games), 4.Nf3 (a quarter) and the Exchange, 4.cxd5 (about one in six).}
4. Bg5 {[%cal Rg5f6] White pins the knight that guards d5.} Be7 {Breaks the pin, so the knight can move again, and prepares to castle.}
5. e3 O-O 6. Nf3 {5.Nf3 O-O 6.e3 reaches the same position.} h6 {[%cal Rh6g5] Puts the question to the bishop. 7.Bxf6 Bxf6 gives Black the bishop pair, and after 7.Bh4 Black can play ...b6 without worrying about the pin. The pawn on h6 also gives Black's king an escape square later.}
7. Bh4 b6 {[%cal Gc8b7,Gc7c5] The Tartakower. Next comes ...Bb7, and later ...c5 to free the game. The plans are on the next page.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Plans for both sides"]
[Type "ideas"]
[FEN "rnbq1rk1/p1p1bpp1/1p2pn1p/3p4/2PP3B/2N1PN2/PP3PPP/R2QKB1R w KQ - 0 8"]

{[%cal Gc8b7,Gc7c5,Gf6e4,Gb8d7,Rc4d5,Ra1c1,Rf1d3] [%csl Yd5] The Tartakower position after 7...b6. The green arrows are Black's plans, the red arrows White's. Stockfish rates it as equal (+0.1).

Black's plan. ...Bb7 puts the bishop on the long diagonal, behind the pawn on d5. ...Nbd7 develops the last minor piece, and ...c5 hits White's center. When White's pieces allow it, ...Ne4 trades pieces and frees Black's game.

White's main tries, and our answers:

8.cxd5, four games in ten: take back with the knight, 8...Nxd5. The trades 9.Bxe7 Qxe7 10.Nxd5 exd5 free Black's game, and ...Be6 and ...c5 give Black active hanging pawns (next page).

8.Bd3, about one game in four: 8...Bb7 9.O-O Nbd7. Against 10.Rc1, the most common move, 10...dxc4 11.Bxc4 c5 (Stockfish +0.1, equal). Against 10.Qe2, 10...c5, and after 11.Rfd1 or 11.Rad1, 11...Ne4 trades pieces (Stockfish +0.1, equal).

8.Be2, 8.Rc1, 8.Qc2 and 8.Qb3: 8...Bb7 and ...Nbd7, then ...c5, or ...dxc4 and ...c5 once White's bishop has moved. Stockfish rates all of them as equal.

7.Bxf6 instead of 7.Bh4: take back with the bishop. Then ...c6, ...Nd7 and ...dxc4 prepare ...e5 or ...c5, and Stockfish rates the position as equal.

The Exchange (4.cxd5 exd5) fixes the pawn structure, and the plans are different. See Meeting the Exchange.

4.Nf3 Be7 5.Bf4: castle, then 6.e3 c5 7.dxc5 Bxc5 frees the game (Stockfish +0.3, slightly better for White).

The Catalan (3.Nf3 Nf6 4.g3): ...Be7 and castle, then take on c4 once White has castled: 6...dxc4 7.Qc2 a6. Against 8.Qxc4, 8...b5 9.Qc2 Bb7 (Stockfish +0.1, equal); against 8.a4, 8...Bd7 and ...Bc6.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Pawn structures"]
[Type "ideas"]
[FEN "rn3rk1/p3qpp1/4b2p/2pp4/8/3BPN2/PP3PPP/2RQK2R w K - 0 14"]

{[%cal Gd5d4,Gc5c4,Ge6f5,Rc1c5,Rd1d5] [%csl Yc5,Yd5] The hanging pawns. This position comes from 8.cxd5 Nxd5 9.Bxe7 Qxe7 10.Nxd5 exd5 11.Rc1 Be6 12.Bd3 c5 13.dxc5 bxc5. Stockfish rates it as equal (0.0).

Black's pawns on c5 and d5 stand side by side, with no pawns next to them, so pieces must guard them. In return they control b4, c4, d4 and e4, and they can advance.

Keep them side by side. If one pawn is forced forward, the other can become weak. Push only with a purpose: ...d4 opens lines when White's pieces are not ready, and ...c4 gains space when it drives White's bishop back. For example, 13.O-O instead of 13.dxc5 allows 13...c4! 14.Bb1, and Black's queenside pawns have more room (Stockfish 0.0, equal).

Bishop on e6, not b7. Once the d5 pawn is fixed, a bishop on b7 bites on its own pawn. On e6 it guards d5 and can come to f5 or g4.

Take back with the pawn. After 13.dxc5, recapture 13...bxc5. A piece on c5 would leave Black with an isolated pawn on d5 and give White the d4 square, and here 13...Qxc5?? even loses the queen to 14.Rxc5 (see Tricks and traps).

Develop around them: ...Nd7, then the knight to f6 or b6, the rooks to c8 and b8, and ...a5.

The Carlsbad structure, after the Exchange: Black's pawns on c6 and d5 face White's on d4 and e3, and Black has the half-open e-file. See Meeting the Exchange.

The isolated pawn. If Black ever recaptures on c5 with a piece, the pawn on d5 is isolated. Then Black needs active pieces, and White aims to blockade d4 and trade pieces.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Tricks and traps"]
[Type "ideas"]
[FEN "r1bqkb1r/pppn1ppp/5n2/3N2B1/3P4/8/PP2PPPP/R2QKBNR b KQkq - 0 6"]

{[%cal Gf6d5,Gf8b4,Rg5d8] Five patterns to know.

The Elephant trap. In the Exchange, after 4.cxd5 exd5 5.Bg5 Nbd7, the pawn on d5 looks free, because the knight on f6 is pinned. White grabs it with 6.Nxd5?? in about one game in five, and this is the position. Play 6...Nxd5! White can take your queen with 7.Bxd8, but 7...Bb4+ forces 8.Qd2 Bxd2+ 9.Kxd2 Kxd8, and you are a piece up (Stockfish −5.1, winning for Black). The trap works only with the knight on d7, which is one reason we play 5...Nbd7.

Take back with the bishop. After 6...h6 7.Bxf6, recapture 7...Bxf6. 7...gxf6? wrecks the pawns in front of your king, and White's pieces aim at it with Bd3 and Nh4 (Stockfish +3.1, winning for White).

Take back with the knight. Against 8.cxd5, play 8...Nxd5. The tempting 8...c5? fails to 9.dxc6, and White has won a pawn (Stockfish +1.8, clearly better for White).

Take back with the pawn. In the hanging-pawn line, after 11.Rc1 Be6 12.Bd3 c5 13.dxc5, recapture 13...bxc5. 13...Qxc5?? loses the queen to 14.Rxc5, because the rook on c1 attacks it (Stockfish +7.5, winning for White). Check which pieces see c5 before you recapture there.

Wait for the bishop. Take on c4 only after White's bishop has left f1. Then Bxc4 costs White a second move with the same bishop, and you gain a tempo. In the Tartakower, after 8.Bd3, 8...dxc4 9.Bxc4 is fine for Black (Stockfish +0.1, equal); against 8.Rc1 or 8.Qc2, wait.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Meeting the Exchange"]
[Type "ideas"]
[FEN "r1bqrnk1/pp2bppp/2p2n2/3p2B1/3P4/2NBP3/PPQ1NPPP/R4RK1 w - - 8 11"]

{[%cal Gf6e4,Gf8g6,Ge7d6,Gc8e6,Ra1b1,Rb2b4,Rb4b5,Rf2f3] [%csl Yc6] The Exchange Variation from Black's side, after 4.cxd5 exd5 5.Bg5 Nbd7 6.e3 c6 7.Bd3 Be7 8.Qc2 O-O 9.Nge2 Re8 10.O-O Nf8. The green arrows are Black's plans, the red arrows White's. Stockfish rates it as equal (+0.2).

The trade on d5 fixed the pawns: White's d4 and e3 against Black's d5 and c6. It also freed your c8 bishop and gave you the half-open e-file.

Your setup: ...c6 to hold d5, ...Be7, castle, ...Re8, then ...Nf8. The knight on f8 guards h7 and goes on to g6 or e6.

Against the minority attack (Rb1, b4, b5): don't open lines for White. ...a6 makes White spend a move on a4 before b5. Meanwhile play on the kingside, where you have more space: ...Ne4 trades pieces, and ...Ng6 and ...Bd6 aim at h2.

Against f3 and e4: White wants to open the center. Get ready with ...g6 and ...Ne6, which hits d4 and g5.

The c8 bishop: trade it with ...Be6 or ...Bg4 when White allows it, or play ...g6 and ...Bf5. While White's queen and bishop control f5, it often stays on d7 or e6.

If White plays Nf3 instead of Nge2, there is no f3 plan, and White usually goes for the minority attack. Then ...Ng6 and ...Ne4 are your counterplay (Stockfish +0.5, slightly better for White, after 9.Nf3 Re8 10.O-O Nf8).} *

[Chapter "QGD – Tartakower: 8.cxd5"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Tartakower Defense, Makogonov Exchange Variation"]
[ECO "D59"]

{The main line of the course. After 4.Bg5 Be7 5.e3 O-O 6.Nf3 h6 7.Bh4 b6, four White players in ten take on d5 at once. Take back with the knight and trade two pairs of minor pieces: every trade gives Black's pieces more room. Then ...Be6 guards d5, and ...c5 hits White's center. Against castling, ...c4 gains space. If White takes on c5, take back with the pawn and play with hanging pawns (see Key ideas: Pawn structures).}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 {If White starts with 3.Nf3 Nf6 4.Bg5, about one game in thirty, play 4...Be7 and castle. Seven games in ten then reach this chapter: 5.Nc3 O-O is the 5.Nf3 branch below, and 5.e3 O-O 6.Nc3 h6 is the main line after 6...h6. If White plays Bd3 and castles before Nc3, play ...h6, ...b6 and ...Bb7 all the same: 6.Bd3 h6 7.Bh4 b6 8.O-O Bb7 9.Nc3 is QGD – Tartakower: 8.Bd3 after 9.O-O (Stockfish +0.1, equal). Against 6.Nbd2, 6...h6 7.Bh4 b6 (Stockfish 0.0, equal).} Nf6 4. Bg5 Be7 {Breaks the pin and prepares to castle. Against 5.cxd5 or 5.Bxf6, see Advanced variations.}
5. e3
(5. Nf3 O-O 6. e3 h6 {The same position as in the main line after 6...h6. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.})
5... O-O
6. Nf3 {The most common move. Against 6.Bd3 or 6.cxd5, see QGD – Tartakower: 6.Bd3 and 6.cxd5. After 6.cxd5, take back at once: 6...h6? drops a pawn. Against 6.Rc1 or 6.Qc2, play 6...h6 and ...b6 just the same. If 6.Qc2 h6 7.h4, White aims at Black's king: hit back in the center with 7...c5 (Stockfish −0.2, equal).} h6 {[%cal Rh6g5] Puts the question to the bishop. Against 7.Bxf6, see QGD – Tartakower: 7.Bxf6.}
7. Bh4 {Against 7.Bf4, about one game in fourteen, strike in the center with 7...c5, as in QGD – Three Knights: 5.Bf4. After 8.cxd5 Nxd5 9.Nxd5, take back with the queen: 9...Qxd5 10.dxc5 Qxc5 (Stockfish −0.2, equal). 9...cxd4?? loses a piece to 10.Nxe7+ (Stockfish +6.7).} b6 {[%cal Gc8b7,Gc7c5] The Tartakower: the bishop comes out next, then ...c5.}
8. cxd5 Nxd5 {This is the Makogonov Exchange Variation. Take back with the knight: it offers trades.}
(8... c5? {Natural, but White's pawn on d5 can take it en passant.} 9. dxc6 {White has won a pawn, and Black's center is gone. Stockfish: +1.8, clearly better for White.})
9. Bxe7 Qxe7 {After 9.Nxd5 exd5 10.Bxe7 Qxe7, the same position arises.}
10. Nxd5 exd5 {[%csl Yd5] The c8 bishop is free again, and Black has the half-open e-file.}
11. Bd3 {The most common move. 11.Rc1 is under Advanced variations.} Be6 {[%cal Ge6d5] The bishop guards d5. With the pawn fixed on d5, a bishop on b7 would bite on its own pawn.}
12. O-O c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Black's freeing break.}
13. Rc1 {The most common move. After 13.dxc5 bxc5, Black has active hanging pawns on c5 and d5 (Stockfish 0.0, equal).} c4 {[%cal Gc4b3] Gains space on the queenside and drives the bishop back.}
14. Bb1 Nc6 {The last minor piece comes out. If 15.Ne5, trade it with 15...Nxe5. Stockfish: 0.0, equal.} *

[Chapter "QGD – Tartakower: 8.Bd3"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Tartakower Defense"]
[ECO "D58"]

{About one White player in four develops with 8.Bd3. Finish development with ...Bb7 and ...Nbd7, then take on c4: White's bishop has already moved once, so Bxc4 costs White a tempo. Then ...c5 frees the game.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Be7 5. e3 O-O 6. Nf3 h6 7. Bh4 b6
8. Bd3 Bb7 {[%cal Gb7d5] The bishop goes to the long diagonal, behind the pawn on d5.}
9. O-O {Against 9.cxd5, take back with the knight: 9...Nxd5 trades pieces (Stockfish −0.1, equal).} Nbd7
10. Rc1 {The most common move. Against 10.Qe2, play 10...c5: after 11.Rfd1 or 11.Rad1, 11...Ne4 trades pieces (Stockfish +0.1, equal). Against 10.cxd5, 10...exd5 is the simple recapture (Stockfish +0.3, slightly better for White).} dxc4 {White's bishop moved to d3, and now it must move again to take back.}
11. Bxc4 c5 {[%cal Gc5d4,Gb7g2] Black hits the center, and the bishop on b7 eyes the long diagonal. Next ...cxd4 and ...a6. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.} *

[Chapter "QGD – Tartakower: 7.Bxf6"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Anti-Tartakower Variation"]
[ECO "D55"]

{About one White player in seven takes on f6 before Black can play ...b6. Take back with the bishop: Black gets the bishop pair, and the bishop on f6 presses on d4. Wait for White's bishop to move, then take on c4 and play ...c5.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Be7 5. e3 O-O 6. Nf3 h6
7. Bxf6 {This is the Anti-Tartakower Variation.} Bxf6 {Always take back with the bishop.}
(7... gxf6? {Wrecks the pawns in front of Black's king.} 8. cxd5 exd5 9. Bd3 {[%cal Gd3h7,Gd1h5] White's pieces aim at the open king, and Qc2 or Nh4 comes next. Stockfish: +2.9, winning for White.})
8. Bd3 {The most common move.

Against 8.cxd5, take back with the e-pawn: 8...exd5, then ...c6, ...Be6 and ...Nd7 (Stockfish +0.1, equal). 8...c5? runs into 9.dxc6, the same trick as in the main Tartakower (Stockfish +1.0). Against 8.Rc1, play 8...c6 and ...Nd7 (Stockfish +0.1, equal).} dxc4 {White's bishop has just moved, so Bxc4 costs it a second move.}
9. Bxc4 c5 {[%cal Gc5d4,Gf6d4] The freeing break. The bishop on f6 adds pressure on d4.}
10. O-O cxd4 {After 11.exd4, White has an isolated pawn on d4, and Black's bishop on f6 hits it. After 11.Nxd4, develop with ...Bd7 and ...Nc6. Stockfish: −0.2, equal.} *

[Chapter "QGD – Exchange: 4.cxd5"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Exchange Variation, Reshevsky Variation"]
[ECO "D36"]

{About one White player in six takes on d5 at once. The pawns are fixed, and Black's c8 bishop is free. Play 5...Nbd7: it sets the Elephant trap, which catches one White player in five (see the trap chapter). Then ...c6, ...Be7, castle, ...Re8 and ...Nf8. The plans are on Key ideas: Meeting the Exchange.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3
(3. cxd5 exd5 4. Nc3 Nf6 {The same position as after 4.cxd5 exd5 in the main line. Stockfish: +0.2, equal.})
3... Nf6 4. cxd5 exd5 {[%cal Gc8f5] The c8 bishop's diagonal is open again.}
5. Bg5 {[%cal Gg5f6,Rf6d5] Pins the knight that guards d5. Against 5.Nf3 or 5.Bf4, see Advanced variations.} Nbd7 {[%csl Yd5] Now d5 looks loose, but 6.Nxd5?? loses a piece to 6...Nxd5!: see the trap chapter on the Elephant trap.}
6. e3 {Against 6.Nf3, play 6...c6 and the same setup.} c6 {[%cal Gc6d5] Guards d5 for good.}
7. Bd3 Be7 8. Nge2 {The most common move. 8.Qc2 O-O 9.Nge2 reaches the same position. Against 8.Nf3, use the same setup: ...O-O, ...Re8 and ...Nf8 (Stockfish +0.5, slightly better for White).} O-O
9. Qc2 Re8 10. O-O {This is the Reshevsky Variation.} Nf8 {[%cal Gf8g6,Gf8e6] The knight guards h7 and heads for e6 or g6.}
11. f3 {[%cal Ge3e4] White's most common plan: prepare e4. Against 11.Rab1 and the minority attack, see Key ideas: Meeting the Exchange.} g6 {[%cal Gf8e6] Prepares ...Ne6.}
12. Bh4 Ne6 {[%cal Ge6d4,Ge6g5] The knight hits d4 and g5, and Black is ready to meet e4. Stockfish: +0.2, equal.} *

[Chapter "QGD – Three Knights: 5.Bf4"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Harrwitz Attack"]
[ECO "D37"]

{After 4.Nf3 Be7, about three White players in ten put the bishop on f4 instead of g5. Without a pin, Black can hit the center at once with ...c5. Against 7.cxd5, take back with the knight and trade into an equal position with no weaknesses. Against 5.Bg5, play 5...O-O 6.e3 h6 and the Tartakower chapters.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3
(3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 Be7 {The same position as after 4.Nf3 Be7 in the main line. Stockfish: +0.3, slightly better for White.})
3... Nf6 4. Nf3 {Against 4.Bf4, play 4...Be7 and castle. After 5.Nf3 O-O, or 5.e3 O-O 6.Nf3 c5, you are in this chapter. Against 5.c5, see QGD – Harrwitz Attack: 4.Bf4.} Be7 5. Bf4 {This is the Harrwitz Attack.} O-O
6. e3 {6.Nb5?! hits c7, but after 6...a6 7.Nc3 dxc4 Black has gained time and taken the c4 pawn (Stockfish −1.7, clearly better for Black).} c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Strikes at d4 before White has castled.}
7. cxd5 {The most common move. Against 7.dxc5, take back with the bishop: 7...Bxc5 frees the game (Stockfish +0.3, slightly better for White). Against 7.Bd3 or 7.Be2, 7...cxd4 releases the tension (Stockfish 0.0, equal).} Nxd5
8. Nxd5 {Against 8.Bg3, pin the knight with 8...Qa5 (Stockfish −0.4, slightly better for Black).} Qxd5 {Take back with the queen.}
(8... cxd4?? {Looks like a good trade, but the knight on d5 takes on e7 with check.} 9. Nxe7+ Qxe7 10. Qxd4 {White has won a piece. Stockfish: +6.5, winning for White.})
9. dxc5 Qxc5 {Black has no weaknesses and will develop with ...Nc6, ...Rd8 and ...Bd7. Stockfish: −0.1, equal.} *

[Chapter "QGD – Three Knights: 5.e3"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Three Knights Variation"]
[ECO "D37"]

{After 4.Nf3 Be7, about one White player in five plays the quiet 5.e3. The same position often comes from 3.e3 or 4.e3. Castle, wait for White's bishop to move, then take on c4 and play ...c5, as in the Queen's Gambit Accepted.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3
(3. e3 Nf6 4. Nf3 {Against 4.cxd5 exd5, develop with ...Be7, castle and play ...c6, as in QGD – Three Knights: 6.cxd5.} Be7 5. Nc3 {Against 5.Bd3, castle: 6.Nc3 is the main line after 6.Bd3. If 6.O-O, take on c4 now that the bishop has moved: 6...dxc4 7.Bxc4 c5 (Stockfish +0.1, equal).} O-O {The same position as in the main line after 5...O-O. If White plays 4.Nc3 first, 4...Be7 5.Nf3 O-O gets there too. Stockfish: 0.0, equal.})
3... Nf6 4. Nf3
(4. e3 Be7 5. Nf3 {Two White players in three. Against 5.Bd3, castle: 6.Nf3 is the main line after 6.Bd3. If 6.Nge2, take on c4 now that the bishop has moved: 6...dxc4 7.Bxc4 c5 (Stockfish −0.2, equal). Against 5.cxd5 exd5, castle and play ...c6: 6.Bd3 O-O 7.Nf3 c6 is QGD – Three Knights: 6.cxd5.} O-O {The same position as in the main line after 5...O-O. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.})
4... Be7 5. e3 O-O
6. Bd3 {The most common move. Against 6.Be2, take on c4 just the same: 6...dxc4 7.Bxc4 is this line again, nine times in ten. If White castles first, 7.O-O c5 8.Bxc4 a6 reaches the end of this line too (Stockfish +0.1, equal). Against 6.cxd5, take back with the pawn: see QGD – Three Knights: 6.cxd5. 6...c5? runs into 7.dxc6 (Stockfish +1.4).} dxc4 {White's bishop has just moved, so Bxc4 costs it a second move.}
7. Bxc4 c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] The freeing break.}
8. O-O a6 {[%cal Gb7b5] Prepares ...b5, which gains time on the bishop. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.} *

[Chapter "QGD – Catalan: 4.g3"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Catalan Opening: Open Defense, Classical Line"]
[ECO "E05"]

{3.Nf3 comes up in about one game in five, and after 3...Nf6 about one White player in four plays 4.g3, the Catalan. The bishop on g2 aims at b7 and the queenside. Develop with ...Be7 and castle, then take on c4 once White has castled. Use ...a6, then ...b5 and ...Bb7 or ...Bd7 and ...Bc6, to blunt the g2 bishop.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. g3 {This is the Catalan Opening.} Be7 5. Bg2 O-O
6. O-O dxc4 {[%csl Yc4] White has castled, so Black takes: the pawn is hard to win back at once, and White has to spend time on it.}
7. Qc2 {The most common move, with 7.Ne5 (under Advanced variations). The queen wins the pawn back next.} a6 {[%cal Gb7b5] Threatens ...b5, which would hold the pawn.}
8. a4 {Stops ...b5. Against 8.Qxc4, play 8...b5 9.Qc2 Bb7: the bishop opposes White's on g2 (Stockfish +0.1, equal).} Bd7 {[%cal Gd7c6] Heads for c6, where it blunts White's bishop on the long diagonal.}
9. Qxc4 Bc6 10. Bg5 Nbd7 {Black has finished development, and the bishop on c6 holds the long diagonal. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.} *

[Chapter "QGD – Tartakower: 11.Rc1"]
[Diagram "11.Rc1"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Tartakower Defense"]
[ECO "D59"]

{About one White player in six puts the rook on the c-file at once. Play 11...Be6 and ...c5, as in the main line. After 13.dxc5, Black gets hanging pawns: take back with the pawn, never the queen. Against 12.Qa4, which eyes a7 and c6, 12...c5 is still the answer.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Be7 5. e3 O-O 6. Nf3 h6 7. Bh4 b6 8. cxd5 Nxd5 9. Bxe7 Qxe7 10. Nxd5 exd5
11. Rc1 Be6
12. Bd3
(12. Qa4 c5 {Black's freeing break. Next ...Rc8 or ...a5. Stockfish: 0.0, equal.})
12... c5 13. dxc5 bxc5 {[%csl Yc5,Yd5] Hanging pawns on c5 and d5: they need care, but they control the center and can advance (see Key ideas: Pawn structures). Stockfish: 0.0, equal.}
(13... Qxc5?? {Takes back on the c-file, where White's rook stands.} 14. Rxc5 {White wins the queen for a rook. Stockfish: +7.5, winning for White.}) *

[Chapter "QGD – Tartakower: other eighth moves"]
[Diagram "8.Be2"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Tartakower Defense"]
[ECO "D58"]

{Three quieter eighth moves, each in about one game in ten: 8.Be2, 8.Rc1 and 8.Qc2. Play ...Bb7 and ...Nbd7. If White takes on d5, take back with the knight and trade. Then break with ...c5.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Be7 5. e3 O-O 6. Nf3 h6 7. Bh4 b6
8. Be2
(8. Rc1 Bb7 9. cxd5 {The most common follow-up.} Nxd5 10. Bxe7 Qxe7 {Two pairs of pieces will come off, as in the main line. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.})
(8. Qc2 Bb7 9. cxd5 Nxd5 10. Bxe7 Qxe7 {The same idea: trade pieces, then ...c5. Stockfish: −0.1, equal.})
8... Bb7 9. O-O Nbd7 10. Rc1 c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Black's freeing break. If White takes on d5, take back with the knight. Stockfish: 0.0, equal.} *

[Chapter "QGD – Tartakower: 6.Bd3 and 6.cxd5"]
[Diagram "6.Bd3"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined"]
[ECO "D53"]

{After 4.Bg5 Be7 5.e3 O-O, about one White player in ten plays 6.Bd3 before Nf3, and about one in seventeen takes on d5 at once with 6.cxd5. Against 6.Bd3, keep the Tartakower setup: ...h6, ...b6 and ...Bb7. Then take on c4 once White's bishop has moved. Against 6.cxd5, take back at once with the knight and trade pieces, as against 8.cxd5 in the main line. Here the usual ...h6 would drop a pawn.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Be7 5. e3 O-O
6. Bd3 {White develops the bishop before the king's knight. Play ...h6 and ...b6 as usual. If White's knight goes to f3 later, you are often back in the main Tartakower chapters.}
(6. cxd5 Nxd5 {Take back with the knight. It offers trades, and every trade gives Black's pieces more room. Most club players take back with the e-pawn, which is also playable (Stockfish +0.3, slightly better for White), but the knight keeps the plan of the main line.}
(6... h6? {White has just taken a pawn, and Black must take it back now.} 7. Bxf6 Bxf6 8. dxe6 Bxe6 {The knight that could have taken back on d5 is gone, and White's pawn took a second pawn on e6. White is a pawn up. Stockfish: +1.2, clearly better for White.})
(6... c5? {The same trick as 8...c5? in the main line: White's pawn on d5 takes it en passant.} 7. dxc6 {White has won a pawn. Stockfish: +1.3, clearly better for White.})
7. Bxe7 Qxe7
8. Bd3 {The most common move, about four times in ten. Against 8.Nxd5 exd5, the c8 bishop is free again, as in the main line (Stockfish 0.0, equal). Against 8.Nf3, trade knights with 8...Nxc3 9.bxc3, then ...b6 and ...Bb7 (Stockfish 0.0, equal).} Nxc3 {Trades a second pair of pieces.}
9. bxc3 c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Hits White's center before White has castled.}
10. Nf3 b6 11. O-O Bb7 {[%cal Gb7f3] The Tartakower setup after all. Next come ...Nd7 and ...Rc8. Stockfish: 0.0, equal.})
6... h6 7. Bh4 {Three White players in four. Against 7.Bxf6 Bxf6 8.Nf3, you are in QGD – Tartakower: 7.Bxf6. Against 7.Bf4, hit the center with 7...c5 (Stockfish 0.0, equal).} b6 {[%cal Gc8b7] The bishop goes to b7 next.}
8. Nge2 {One White player in four puts the knight on e2, where it doesn't block the f-pawn. The most common move, 8.Nf3, reaches QGD – Tartakower: 8.Bd3. Against 8.cxd5, take back with the knight: 8...Nxd5 9.Bxe7 Qxe7 10.Nxd5 exd5, then ...c5, as in the main line (Stockfish +0.1, equal).} Bb7
9. O-O {Seven times in ten.} dxc4 {White's bishop has already moved once, so Bxc4 costs it a second move.}
10. Bxc4 c5 {[%cal Gc5d4,Gb7g2] Black hits the center, and the bishop on b7 looks down the long diagonal. Stockfish: −0.1, equal.} *

[Chapter "QGD – Tartakower: 5.cxd5 and 5.Bxf6"]
[Diagram "5.cxd5"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Exchange Variation, Positional Variation"]
[ECO "D35"]

{Two fifth-move tries, each in about one game in eighteen. After 5.cxd5 exd5, the pawns are the same as in the Exchange Variation: play ...c6 and ...Nbd7 and use the same plans. After 5.Bxf6 Bxf6, Black has the bishop pair: castle and hit the center with ...c5.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Be7
5. cxd5
(5. Bxf6 {This is the Heral Variation.} Bxf6 6. e3 {Against 6.cxd5, take back with the e-pawn: 6...exd5 7.e3 c6 (Stockfish −0.1, equal).} O-O 7. Nf3 c5 {[%cal Gc5d4,Gf6d4] The bishop on f6 supports the break. Stockfish: 0.0, equal.})
5... exd5 6. e3 {Two White players in three. Against 6.Nf3, play 6...c6: 7.e3 Bf5 reaches the end of QGD – Exchange: 5.Nf3 and 5.Bf4 (Stockfish +0.1, equal). If 7.Qc2 keeps the bishop off f5, prepare it with 7...g6 and ...Bf5 (Stockfish 0.0, equal). Against 6.Qc2, play 6...c6 and the Exchange setup (Stockfish +0.2, equal).} c6 7. Bd3 Nbd7 {Next ...Nf8 and ...Ne6, or castle, as in the Exchange chapter. Stockfish: +0.3, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "QGD – Exchange: 5.Nf3 and 5.Bf4"]
[Diagram "5.Nf3"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Exchange Variation"]
[ECO "D35"]

{Without the pin on f6, Black can bring the c8 bishop to f5. Play ...c6 to guard d5, then ...Be7 and ...Bf5 (or ...Bf5 at once against 5.Bf4), and castle.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. cxd5 exd5
5. Nf3
(5. Bf4 c6 6. e3 Bf5 {[%cal Gf5d3] Offers to trade Black's problem bishop.} 7. Bd3 Bxd3 8. Qxd3 Be7 {The light-squared bishops are gone. Castle next. Stockfish: 0.0, equal.})
5... c6 6. Bg5 {Almost six White players in ten. Against 6.Bf4, 6...Bf5 7.e3 Be7 reaches the end of QGD – Three Knights: 5.cxd5 and 5.g3 (Stockfish +0.1, equal). Against 6.e3, put the bishop on f5 at once, and trade it if White offers: 6...Bf5 7.Bd3 Bxd3 8.Qxd3 Bd6 (Stockfish −0.3, slightly better for Black). Against 6.g3, 6...Bf5 too (Stockfish −0.1, equal).} Be7 7. e3 Bf5 {[%cal Gf5d3] The bishop takes its best square, since no White queen covers f5. If 8.Nh4, retreat to e6. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.} *

[Chapter "QGD – Harrwitz Attack: 4.Bf4"]
[Diagram "5.c5"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Harrwitz Attack"]
[ECO "D35"]

{About one game in thirty goes 3.Nc3 Nf6 4.Bf4. Play 4...Be7 and castle. About half the time White adds Nf3, and you are in QGD – Three Knights: 5.Bf4. This chapter covers the space grab c4–c5, which takes the ...c5 break away from Black. Hit the head of White's pawn chain at once with ...b6. When White holds c5 with b4, undermine that pawn with ...a5.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bf4 {This is the Harrwitz Attack. The bishop comes out before e3, but unlike Bg5, it doesn't pin the knight on f6.} Be7 {Castle next. Stockfish's first choice is 4...c5 (0.0, equal), but 4...Be7 leads to QGD – Three Knights: 5.Bf4 in about half of games (Stockfish +0.3, slightly better for White).}
5. c5 {[%cal Gc4c5] About one White player in nine grabs space on the queenside. The pawn on c5 stops ...c5, Black's usual freeing break. Against 5.Nf3 O-O, you are in QGD – Three Knights: 5.Bf4.}
(5. e3 {Almost six White players in ten.} O-O 6. c5 {One White player in twelve grabs space here. Against 6.Nf3, play 6...c5: you are in QGD – Three Knights: 5.Bf4. Against 6.Bd3 or 6.h3, play 6...c5 too (Stockfish 0.0 and +0.1, equal).} b6 {[%cal Gb6c5] Hits the head of the chain, as in the main line.} 7. b4 a5 8. a3 axb4 {The same position as in the main line after 8...axb4. Stockfish: −0.2, equal.})
5... O-O {Castle first. 5...b6 at once is just as good (Stockfish −0.3).}
6. e3 {The most common move, chosen by half of White players. Against 6.h3 or 6.Nf3, play 6...b6 just the same (Stockfish −0.5 and −0.3, slightly better for Black).} b6 {[%cal Gb6c5] Attacks the pawn on c5, the head of White's pawn chain.}
7. b4 {Nine White players in ten hold c5 with the b-pawn. After 7.cxb6 axb6, Black's rook gets the half-open a-file, and ...c5 comes next (Stockfish −0.3, slightly better for Black).} a5 {[%cal Ga5b4] Undermines the pawn on b4, which holds up c5.}
8. a3 axb4 9. axb4 Rxa1 {Trades rooks on the open a-file.}
10. Qxa1 Nc6 {[%cal Gc6b4,Ge6e5] The knight hits b4, and ...e5 comes next to strike at White's center. Stockfish: −0.4, slightly better for Black.} *

[Chapter "QGD – Three Knights: 5.cxd5 and 5.g3"]
[Diagram "5.cxd5"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Three Knights Variation"]
[ECO "D37"]

{Two rarer fifth moves after 4.Nf3 Be7. After 5.cxd5 exd5 6.Bf4, play ...c6 and ...Bf5, as against the Exchange; against 6.Bg5, 6...c6 7.e3 Bf5 reaches Exchange: 5.Nf3. After 5.g3, castle and take on c4: a Catalan in which White's knight is already on c3.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Nf3 Be7
5. cxd5
(5. g3 O-O 6. Bg2 dxc4 7. O-O Nc6 {[%cal Gc6d4] The knight hits d4. Stockfish: −0.2, equal.})
5... exd5 6. Bf4 c6 7. e3 Bf5 {[%cal Gf5d3] The same setup as in the Exchange. Castle next. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.} *

[Chapter "QGD – Three Knights: 6.cxd5"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Three Knights Variation"]
[ECO "D37"]

{After 4.Nf3 Be7 5.e3 O-O, about one White player in eight takes on d5. The same pawns can come from 4.e3 Be7 5.cxd5 and from 3.e3 Nf6 4.cxd5. Take back with the pawn: no White bishop pins your knight, and ...exd5 opens the c8 bishop's diagonal. Then ...c6 holds d5, and the bishop comes out: to g4 against Bd3, to f5 against Be2.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Nf3 Be7 5. e3 O-O 6. cxd5 exd5 {[%cal Gc8g4] Take back with the pawn, as three club players in four do. It opens the c8 bishop's diagonal. 6...Nxd5 is fine too (Stockfish +0.1, equal), but it leaves the bishop shut in.}
7. Bd3 {Two White players in three.}
(7. Be2 {Almost one White player in five.} c6 8. O-O Bf5 {[%cal Gf5b1] With White's bishop on e2, no White piece covers f5, and the bishop takes its best square. If 9.Nh4, retreat to e6. Stockfish: −0.1, equal.})
7... c6 {[%cal Gc6d5] Guards d5 for good. The pawns now stand as in the Exchange Variation (see Key ideas: Meeting the Exchange), but here the c8 bishop can still come out.}
8. O-O {Two White players in three. Against 8.h3, which takes g4 away from the bishop, or 8.Qc2, play 8...Re8 and ...Bd6 (Stockfish 0.0, equal).} Bg4 {[%cal Gg4f3] Pins the knight on f3. The bishop is active outside the pawn chain, and half of club players choose this move. Quiet moves such as 8...Nbd7 or 8...Re8 are just as good (Stockfish +0.1, equal).}
9. h3 {Two White players in three.} Bh5 {[%cal Gh5f3] Keeps the pin. If 10.Qb3 hits b7, 10...Bxf3 11.gxf3 Qd7 guards it, and the pawns in front of White's king are doubled. Stockfish: 0.0, equal.} *

[Chapter "QGD – Catalan: 7.Ne5"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Catalan Opening: Open Defense, Classical Line"]
[ECO "E05"]

{About one Catalan player in three wins the pawn back with 7.Ne5. Hit the knight with 7...Nc6: after the trades on c6, material is level and Black's pieces are free.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. g3 Be7 5. Bg2 O-O 6. O-O dxc4
7. Ne5 Nc6 {[%cal Gc6e5] Challenges the knight.}
8. Bxc6
(8. Nxc6 bxc6 {Take back with the pawn. Black keeps the extra pawn on c4. Stockfish: −0.1, equal.})
8... bxc6 9. Nxc6 Qe8 {[%cal Ge8c6] Hits the knight.}
10. Nxe7+ Qxe7 {Material is level, White has given up the g2 bishop, and Black's pieces are free. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.} *

[Chapter "QGD: other third moves"]
[Diagram "3.a3"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined"]
[ECO "D30"]

{Rarer third moves. Against 3.a3, which stops ...Bb4, play 3...Nf6 and the usual setup. After 3.cxd5 exd5 4.Nf3, develop the same way. 3.e3 usually reaches QGD – Three Knights: 5.e3. Against 3.g3, 3...Nf6 4.Bg2 Be7 5.Nf3 O-O reaches the Catalan; against 3.Bf4, 3...Nf6 4.e3 c5 hits the center (Stockfish 0.0, equal).}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. a3
(3. cxd5 exd5 4. Nf3 Nf6 {Develop with ...Be7 or ...Bd6, ...c6 and castle. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.})
3... Nf6 4. e3 Be7 {Castle next, as usual. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.} *

[Chapter "QGD Trap: the Elephant trap"]
[Diagram "6.Nxd5"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Exchange Variation, Positional Variation"]
[ECO "D35"]

{The most rewarding trap in this repertoire. In the Exchange Variation, after 5...Nbd7, the pawn on d5 looks free, because the knight on f6 is pinned. One White player in five grabs it. The pin isn't real: after 6...Nxd5!, White can take Black's queen, but ...Bb4+ wins it back, and Black ends up a piece ahead.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Bg5 Nbd7
6. Nxd5?? {Grabs the pawn.} Nxd5! {Gives up the queen.}
7. Bxd8 {White takes the queen. Other moves leave White a knight down.}
(7. Nf3 Bb4+ 8. Bd2 Bxd2+ 9. Qxd2 N7f6 {Black is a knight up for a pawn. Stockfish: −5.7, winning for Black.})
7... Bb4+ {[%cal Gb4e1] Check, and White has only one way to block.}
8. Qd2 Bxd2+ {[%engine ok] Takes the queen back. 8...Kxd8 is even stronger, because the queen stays pinned and can't escape.}
9. Kxd2 Kxd8 {Black has won a knight for a pawn. Stockfish: −5.1, winning for Black.} *
